        takeaway: 'One repeating cell describes the whole crystal, which is why bulk properties can be computed from atoms.',
        visual: 'unit-cell-concept',
        visualSpec:
          'A large regular array of atoms with one small cell outlined in bold, then that cell translated repeatedly to regenerate the entire array, demonstrating that the cell plus translation is a complete description. Beside it, an amorphous arrangement is drawn with no repeating cell identifiable, annotated accordingly. The lattice parameter is dimensioned on the cell edge. On reveal: the array draws, then one cell outlines, then copies of it translate outward and tile the whole array, then the lattice parameter dimensions, then the amorphous arrangement appears with a crossed-out cell outline.',
        algo: [
          'Confirm the solid has long range repeating order',
          'Identify the smallest volume that reproduces the structure by translation alone',
          'Measure its edge lengths, which are the lattice parameters',
          'Count how many atoms belong to that cell, sharing corner and face atoms appropriately',
          'Use the cell to compute bulk properties such as theoretical density',
        ],
        complexity: { best: 'cubic cells need only one lattice parameter', avg: 'atoms must be shared correctly between adjacent cells', worst: 'low symmetry crystals need six independent parameters', note: 'Amorphous solids such as window glass have no unit cell and cannot be treated this way.' },
        dryRun: {
          input: 'Counting the atoms belonging to a simple cubic unit cell.',
          steps: [
            'A simple cubic cell has an atom at each of its eight corners',
            'Each corner atom is shared between the eight cells meeting at that corner',
            'So each contributes one eighth to this cell',
            'Eight corners times one eighth gives one atom per cell',
          ],
          result: 'One atom per cell. The sharing rule is the step most often got wrong, and it matters because every density and packing calculation depends on the atom count being right.',
        },
        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'CRYSTALLINE: long range repeating order\nAMORPHOUS:   short range order only, NO repeating cell\n\nUNIT CELL: smallest volume that reproduces the whole\n           structure by TRANSLATION alone\n\nAtom sharing rules:\n  CORNER atom  shared by 8 cells -> contributes 1/8\n  FACE atom    shared by 2 cells -> contributes 1/2\n  EDGE atom    shared by 4 cells -> contributes 1/4\n  INTERIOR     belongs entirely  -> contributes 1\n\nSimple cubic: 8 corners x 1/8 = 1 atom per cell\n\nGet the atom COUNT right: every packing factor and\ndensity calculation depends on it.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.3, pp.48-56 (10th ed. supplied).' },
        mistake: 'Students count every atom touching the cell as belonging to it. Corner atoms are shared between eight cells and face atoms between two, so the effective count is far lower than the number visible.',
