        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'DISLOCATIONS (line defects):\n\n  EDGE   an extra HALF PLANE of atoms, terminating\n         inside the crystal; its edge is the dislocation line\n  SCREW  a shear ramping the planes into a HELIX\n  MIXED  most real dislocations are a combination\n\n  BURGERS VECTOR: magnitude and direction of the\n                  lattice distortion\n\nWHY THEY MATTER:\n\n  rigid whole-plane sliding: ALL bonds break at once\n    -> theoretical strength ~ E/10\n  dislocation motion: ONE ROW of bonds at a time\n    -> real yield stress ~ 1/1000 of theoretical\n\n  (like moving a rug by pushing a ripple along it,\n   rather than dragging the whole rug at once)\n\nSTRENGTHENING = IMPEDING DISLOCATION MOTION (Module 3).\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.4, pp.100-106 (10th ed. supplied).' },
        mistake: 'Students think dislocations are flaws that weaken a metal and should be eliminated. They do lower the yield stress far below the theoretical value, but without them metals would be brittle, and controlling their motion is how every strengthening mechanism works.',
