        definition:
          'Interfacial defects are two dimensional boundaries separating regions of different orientation or structure. The most important is the grain boundary, the surface between two crystals of different orientation within a polycrystalline solid, across which the atomic arrangement is disrupted. Volume defects are three dimensional and include pores, cracks and foreign inclusions, generally introduced during processing. Grain boundaries impede dislocation motion, so a finer grain size gives a stronger material, which is one of the few strengthening mechanisms that improves toughness at the same time.',
        takeaway: 'Grain boundaries block dislocations, so finer grains mean stronger and tougher — a rare combination.',
        visual: 'grain-structure-and-defects',
        visualSpec:
          'A polycrystalline structure with several grains of different lattice orientations, the boundaries between them highlighted and magnified to show the disrupted atomic arrangement. A dislocation is shown gliding within one grain and being halted at the boundary because the slip planes do not line up across it. Beside this, a coarse grained and a fine grained sample are compared with their strengths, the finer being higher. A volume defect panel shows a pore and an inclusion with their stress concentration fields. On reveal: the grain structure draws with its orientations, then a boundary magnifies showing the disruption, then a dislocation glides and halts at it, then the coarse and fine grained strength comparison builds, then the volume defect panel shows the pore and inclusion with their stress fields.',
