      'Built from TB1 Chapter 4 sections on microscopy and Chapter 5 on diffusion, which the syllabus names in substance. The two halves are placed in one module for a good reason: microscopy is how the results of diffusion are observed, and a carburised case is the standard example used in both. The organising idea in the microscopy half is that nothing is visible until it has been made visible, so specimen preparation and etching receive as much attention as the instruments themselves. The organising idea in the diffusion half is that atoms move by discrete hops that require a vacancy or an interstitial site, so the exponential temperature dependence of the hopping rate propagates directly into every practical heat treatment time. All page numbers are from the TENTH edition supplied; see the edition notice in the file header.',
