        terms: ['microstructure', 'grain', 'phase', 'inclusion', 'failure analysis'],
        definition:
          'Microstructure is the arrangement of grains, phases and defects visible at magnifications between roughly ten and one hundred thousand times, and it is the level at which the structure of the materials science chain is actually observed. Two specimens of identical chemical composition can have entirely different microstructures and therefore entirely different properties, so composition analysis alone cannot predict behaviour. Microscopic examination is used to verify that a heat treatment produced the intended structure, to measure grain size, to identify phases present and their proportions, and in failure analysis to determine why a component broke.',
        takeaway: 'Composition does not determine properties; microstructure does, and only microscopy reveals it.',
