        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'TRANSMISSION ELECTRON MICROSCOPY (TEM):\n\n  electrons pass THROUGH the specimen\n  -> specimen must be thinned to a FOIL, << 100 nm\n\n  CONTRAST MECHANISM: DIFFRACTION\n    a dislocation bends the lattice locally\n    -> that region diffracts the beam differently\n    -> appears as a LINE on the image\n\n  resolution: ATOMIC scale\n  -> the ONLY technique that images INDIVIDUAL DISLOCATIONS\n  -> also images fine precipitates\n\n  COSTS: thinning is slow and skilled\n         a foil thin enough to transmit may have LOST\n         dislocations to its free surfaces\n         -> may not represent the bulk\n\nSEM contrast = TOPOGRAPHY.  TEM contrast = DIFFRACTION.\nThat is the fundamental difference.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.4, pp.113-118 (10th ed. supplied).' },
        mistake: 'Students assume a thinner foil is always better because it transmits more electrons. Thinning also allows dislocations to escape to the free surfaces, so an excessively thin foil can show a dislocation density well below that of the bulk material.',
