        terms: ['feature size', 'surface or interior', 'depth of field', 'preparation effort', 'selection'],
        definition:
          'The three techniques answer different questions and are selected by matching the instrument to the feature. Optical microscopy suits features above about two hundred nanometres on a flat prepared section, which covers grains, phases and inclusions and therefore most routine work. Scanning electron microscopy suits surfaces requiring high depth of field, above all fracture surfaces, and resolves to a few nanometres. Transmission electron microscopy suits features requiring atomic resolution within the material, principally dislocations and fine precipitates, at the cost of demanding specimen preparation.',
