        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'VACANCY (SUBSTITUTIONAL) DIFFUSION:\n\n  an atom on a NORMAL lattice site exchanges places\n  with an ADJACENT VACANCY\n\n  TWO requirements, BOTH must be met:\n    1. a VACANCY must be adjacent\n       (concentration rises exponentially with T)\n    2. the atom must have enough THERMAL ENERGY to\n       squeeze past its neighbours (saddle point barrier)\n       (energetic fraction also exponential in T)\n\n  -> activation energy includes BOTH vacancy formation\n     AND motion\n  -> HIGH Q  -> extreme temperature sensitivity\n\n  SELF DIFFUSION (host atoms among themselves) uses\n  the same mechanism.\n\nAt room temperature, substitutional diffusion in steel\nis effectively FROZEN.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.5, pp.126-128 (10th ed. supplied).' },
        mistake: 'Students assume an atom can move whenever it has enough thermal energy. It also needs somewhere to move into, and the requirement for an adjacent vacancy is what makes substitutional diffusion so much slower than interstitial.',
