        code: { lang: 'text', src: "FICK'S FIRST LAW (STEADY STATE):\n\n  J = -D * (dC/dx)\n\n  J = flux, mass per unit AREA per unit TIME (kg/m^2.s)\n  D = diffusion coefficient (m^2/s)\n  dC/dx = concentration gradient (kg/m^3 per m)\n\n  NEGATIVE SIGN: flux runs DOWN the gradient\n\nSTEADY STATE means concentration at every point is\nCONSTANT IN TIME -> the gradient does not change\n\nThin membrane example (linear profile):\n  dC/dx = (C_A - C_B) / thickness\n\n  2 mm sheet, 1.2 -> 0.8 kg/m^3, D = 3e-11 m^2/s\n  gradient = 0.4/0.002 = 200\n  J = 3e-11 * 200 = 6e-9 kg/m^2.s\n\nDOES NOT APPLY when concentrations change with time.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.5, pp.128-131 (10th ed. supplied)." },
