        definition:
          'Designing a practical diffusion treatment means selecting a temperature and a time that together produce the required concentration at the required depth. Because the diffusion coefficient is exponential in temperature while depth goes as the square root of time, raising the temperature is far more effective than extending the time. The limit is that high temperature coarsens the grain structure, which lowers strength and toughness, so the cycle is a compromise between furnace time and the metallurgical damage that haste would cause.',
        takeaway: 'Raising temperature beats extending time, until grain coarsening stops you.',
