        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'DESIGNING A DIFFUSION CYCLE:\n\n  requirement: concentration C_x at depth x\n\n  1. pick a trial temperature T\n  2. D = D_0 * exp(-Q_d/RT)\n  3. from the error function solution, find t\n  4. CHECK T against the GRAIN COARSENING limit\n  5. iterate\n\nWHY RAISE TEMPERATURE RATHER THAN EXTEND TIME:\n\n  D is EXPONENTIAL in T\n  x goes only as SQRT(t)\n\n  1.0 mm case: 16 h at 900 C\n                ~5.5 h at 1000 C (D roughly triples)\n\nTHE LIMIT: high T COARSENS the grain structure\n  -> lower strength AND toughness\n  -> the cycle is a COMPROMISE between furnace time\n     and metallurgical damage\n  -> a grain refining treatment AFTER carburising can\n     sometimes recover the structure.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.5, pp.131-141 (10th ed. supplied).' },
