        definition:
          'Elastic deformation is recoverable: the specimen returns to its original dimensions when the load is removed. In this region stress is proportional to strain, with the constant of proportionality being the modulus of elasticity, and the relation is Hooke law. Physically the strain corresponds to stretching interatomic bonds without breaking any of them, which is why the modulus correlates closely with bonding strength and why it is barely affected by processing. Two steels of very different strength have essentially the same modulus, because heat treatment changes the defect structure but not the bonding.',
