        definition:
          'Selecting which property governs a design begins with the loading history rather than with the material. A single monotonic load is governed by yield strength, with toughness relevant if impact or a notch is present. Repeated loading is governed by fatigue behaviour, where the endurance limit applies for ferrous alloys and a finite life calculation otherwise. Sustained loading at elevated temperature is governed by creep. Stiffness requirements are governed by the modulus, which no heat treatment will change. The commonest design error is applying a tensile test result to a duty the tensile test never examined.',
        takeaway: 'Start from the loading history: monotonic, cyclic or sustained each has its own governing property.',
