        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'SOLID SOLUTION: solute dissolves into the solvent\ncrystal structure WITHOUT changing that structure.\n\n  SUBSTITUTIONAL  solute on NORMAL lattice sites\n  INTERSTITIAL    solute in the GAPS (small atoms only)\n\nHUME-ROTHERY RULES for COMPLETE substitutional solubility:\n\n  1. atomic radii within about 15%\n  2. SAME crystal structure\n  3. similar ELECTRONEGATIVITY\n  4. same VALENCE\n\n  ALL FOUR satisfied -> single phase at EVERY composition\n  ANY violated       -> SOLUBILITY LIMIT; beyond it a\n                        SECOND PHASE appears\n\nCu-Ni: radii 0.128 vs 0.125 nm (2%), both FCC,\n       electronegativity 1.9 vs 1.8, both valence +2\n  -> ALL FOUR met -> complete solubility\n  -> the standard ISOMORPHOUS example\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.9, pp.251-255 (10th ed. supplied;\nsee the edition notice in the file header).' },
        mistake: 'Students apply the Hume-Rothery rules to interstitial solutes. Those rules govern substitution on lattice sites; an interstitial atom occupies a gap instead, so only its size relative to that gap matters.',
