        terms: ['ferrite', 'austenite', 'cementite', 'eutectoid', 'allotropy'],
        definition:
          'The iron-iron carbide diagram underlies every steel and cast iron. Iron is allotropic, existing as body-centred cubic ferrite at low temperature, face-centred cubic austenite above about 912 degrees Celsius, and body-centred cubic delta ferrite near melting. Austenite dissolves far more carbon than ferrite because its interstitial sites are larger. Cementite is the iron carbide compound at 6.7 percent carbon. Three invariant reactions occur, of which the eutectoid at 727 degrees and 0.76 percent carbon is by far the most important, since austenite transforms there into pearlite.',
        takeaway: 'Iron changes structure with temperature, austenite holds more carbon, and the eutectoid reaction makes pearlite.',
        visual: 'fe-fe3c-diagram',
        visualSpec:
          'The iron-iron carbide diagram from zero to 6.7 percent carbon with all phase fields labelled: delta, austenite, ferrite, cementite and their combinations. The three invariant reactions are marked with horizontal isotherms: peritectic at the top, eutectic in the middle at 4.3 percent carbon, and eutectoid at 727 degrees and 0.76 percent carbon, the last highlighted as the critical one. A carbon solubility inset compares the small interstitial site in body-centred cubic ferrite against the larger one in face-centred cubic austenite. On reveal: the diagram draws its fields in sequence, then the three isotherms appear with their reactions written, then the eutectoid highlights, then the solubility inset compares the two interstitial sites.',
        algo: [
          'Note that iron is allotropic, changing crystal structure with temperature',
          'Identify ferrite as body-centred cubic with very low carbon solubility',
          'Identify austenite as face-centred cubic with much higher carbon solubility',
          'Identify cementite as the carbide compound at 6.7 percent carbon',
          'Locate the eutectoid reaction at 727 degrees and 0.76 percent carbon',
        ],
        complexity: { best: 'the eutectoid reaction alone explains most steel heat treatment', avg: 'three invariant reactions are present in the full diagram', worst: 'the region above the eutectic concerns cast irons and adds further complexity', note: 'Steels contain less than about two percent carbon; above that the alloy is a cast iron.' },
        dryRun: {
          input: 'Why austenite dissolves far more carbon than ferrite despite being more densely packed.',
          steps: [
            'Face-centred cubic austenite has a packing factor of 0.74 against 0.68 for body-centred cubic ferrite',
            'So austenite has less total free space, which suggests it should hold less carbon',
            'However the free space in austenite is concentrated into fewer but larger interstitial sites',
            'Carbon fits into those larger sites with far less lattice strain than into the small ones in ferrite',
          ],
          result: 'Austenite dissolves roughly a hundred times more carbon despite being more densely packed. It is the size of the individual interstitial site rather than the total free volume that governs, and this single fact underlies all steel heat treatment.',
        },
        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'IRON-IRON CARBIDE DIAGRAM - the basis of all steels.\n\nIRON IS ALLOTROPIC:\n  delta ferrite  BCC, near melting\n  AUSTENITE      FCC, above ~912 C\n  FERRITE        BCC, below ~912 C\n\nPHASES:\n  FERRITE (alpha)  BCC, max ~0.022% C at 727 C\n  AUSTENITE (gamma) FCC, max ~2.14% C at 1147 C\n  CEMENTITE (Fe3C)  compound, 6.70% C, hard and brittle\n\nWHY AUSTENITE HOLDS ~100x MORE CARBON despite being\nMORE densely packed (APF 0.74 vs 0.68):\n  its free space is in FEWER but LARGER interstitial\n  sites -> carbon fits with far less lattice strain\n  -> SITE SIZE governs, not total free volume\n  -> this single fact underlies ALL steel heat treatment\n\nTHREE INVARIANT REACTIONS - the vital one:\n  EUTECTOID  727 C, 0.76% C:\n    austenite -> ferrite + cementite  (= PEARLITE)\n\nSTEEL: < ~2.14% C.   CAST IRON: above that.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.9, pp.296-302 (10th ed. supplied).' },
        mistake: 'Students expect the more loosely packed body-centred cubic ferrite to dissolve more carbon because it has more free space. The free space in austenite is concentrated into larger individual sites, and it is site size rather than total volume that determines interstitial solubility.',
        book: { chapter: 9, pages: '296-302' },
        bloom: 'L5',
        co: 'CO4',
