        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'MICROSTRUCTURE DEVELOPMENT IN STEELS (slow cooling):\n\n  EUTECTOID (0.76% C):\n    austenite -> entirely PEARLITE at 727 C\n    (lamellar ferrite + cementite plates)\n\n  HYPOEUTECTOID (< 0.76% C):\n    1. PROEUTECTOID FERRITE nucleates at austenite\n       GRAIN BOUNDARIES\n    2. ferrite holds almost no C -> remaining austenite\n       ENRICHES in carbon, moving RIGHT to 0.76%\n    3. at 727 C that austenite -> PEARLITE\n    -> ferrite grains + pearlite\n\n  HYPEREUTECTOID (> 0.76% C):\n    PROEUTECTOID CEMENTITE forms a CONTINUOUS NETWORK\n    around prior austenite grains -> VERY BRITTLE\n    -> broken up by SPHEROIDISING before machining\n\nLEVER RULE just ABOVE 727 C gives the proeutectoid\nfraction; the remainder becomes pearlite.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.9, pp.296-302 (10th ed. supplied).' },
