        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'COMPOSITE: a deliberately made multiphase material\n\n  MATRIX          continuous phase; transfers load,\n                  holds alignment, protects\n  DISPERSED PHASE discontinuous; usually carries the load\n\nCLASSIFIED BY THE GEOMETRY OF THE DISPERSED PHASE:\n\n  PARTICLE REINFORCED   roughly EQUIAXED particles\n      large particle    (concrete, cermets)\n      dispersion strengthened (fine, sub-micron)\n\n  FIBRE REINFORCED      HIGH aspect ratio\n      by fibre LENGTH   (continuous / discontinuous)\n      by ORIENTATION    (aligned / random)\n\n  STRUCTURAL            property from the ARRANGEMENT\n      LAMINAR           (plywood, cross-plied laminates)\n      SANDWICH PANEL    (stiff faces, light core)\n\nCONCRETE is particle reinforced and is by VOLUME the\nmost used composite in the world.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.16, pp.564-570 (10th ed. supplied;\nsee the edition notice in the file header).' },
