        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'CERAMICS - metal + non-metal, IONIC and/or COVALENT bonds.\nMay be CRYSTALLINE or AMORPHOUS.\n\n  bonds STRONG      -> HARD, STIFF, high melting point,\n                       chemically INERT, REFRACTORY\n  bonds DIRECTIONAL -> NO slip mechanism -> BRITTLE\n\nFLAW SENSITIVITY:\n  no plastic zone to blunt a crack tip\n  -> strength set by the LARGEST FLAW present\n  -> tensile strength SCATTERS widely between nominally\n     identical specimens (a STATISTICAL quantity)\n  -> compressive strength is MUCH higher: compression\n     CLOSES flaws instead of opening them\n\nDESIGN RULE: KEEP CERAMICS IN COMPRESSION.\n\n  furnace linings  compression from self weight + inertness\n  cutting tools    hardness + hot hardness, edge in compression\n  bearings         hardness + wear under compressive contact\n  tie rods         AVOID - pure tension opens flaws\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.12, pp.405-441 (10th ed. supplied).' },
