        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'GLASSES - NON-CRYSTALLINE ceramics (amorphous):\n\n  silica (SiO2) network, no long range order\n  MODIFIER oxides (Na2O, CaO) BREAK network links\n  -> lower working temperature, easier forming\n\nNO SHARP MELTING POINT:\n  viscosity falls CONTINUOUSLY with temperature\n  process points defined by VISCOSITY:\n    working point, softening point, annealing point,\n    strain point\n  -> formed by VISCOSITY CONTROL, not by melting\n\nSTRENGTH is governed by SURFACE FLAWS.\n\n  ANNEALING  slow cool -> relieves internal stress\n  TEMPERING  rapid surface cool -> SURFACE in residual\n             COMPRESSION, interior in tension\n             -> applied tension must FIRST overcome that\n                compression -> much higher usable strength\n\n  CONSEQUENCE: breach the compressive layer (scratch,\n  impact, cutting) and the stored energy shatters the\n  WHOLE pane into small fragments.\n  -> tempered glass CANNOT be cut after tempering.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.13, pp.442-460 (10th ed. supplied).' },
