        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'ELASTOMERS - very large RECOVERABLE deformation:\n\n  chains are COILED at rest (above their T_g)\n  load  -> chains UNCOIL (not slide) -> huge extension\n  unload-> chains RECOIL\n\n  PROBLEM without cross-links: uncoiled chains also SLIDE\n  past one another -> PERMANENT set\n\n  VULCANISATION: sulphur cross-links tie chains at intervals\n  -> sliding PREVENTED, uncoiling still allowed\n  -> FULL RECOVERY\n  -> more cross-links = stiffer; TOO many = rigid thermoset\n\nFIBRES - the OPPOSITE arrangement:\n\n  chains deliberately ALIGNED along the fibre axis by DRAWING\n  -> the strong COVALENT BACKBONE lies along the load path\n  -> VERY high axial strength and stiffness\n  -> LOW transverse strength (only secondary bonds across)\n\nSAME chains in both cases. Only the ARRANGEMENT and the\nCONSTRAINT differ.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.15, pp.545-563 (10th ed. supplied).' },
